From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Cristian Papa <pcristian292@gmail.com>
Date: Mon, 9 Nov 2026 19:30:00 -0300
Subject: [PATCH] net: airoha: xpon: forget a GEM port OMCI deleted even if the table
 write failed

airoha_gpon_omci_hw_set_gem_port() returned before clearing the port in
omci_gems when writing the GEM table failed. The MIB reset ignores that
error and drops the ME anyway, so the bit stayed set with nothing behind
it, and since 999-59 gpon_restore_omci_gems() enabled that port again on
every OMCC up until a reboot. Clear the bit for a delete whatever the
write returned; the error still reaches the caller.

Found in a review of 999-58..67.

Signed-off-by: Cristian Papa <pcristian292@gmail.com>
Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
---
--- a/drivers/net/ethernet/airoha/airoha_xpon.c
+++ b/drivers/net/ethernet/airoha/airoha_xpon.c
@@ -2843,13 +2843,20 @@ int airoha_gpon_omci_hw_set_gem_port(voi
 	 */
 	ret = __gpon_set_gem_port_hw(priv, gem_port_id, valid, encrypted,
 				     true);
-	if (ret)
+	if (ret && valid)
 		return ret;
 
-	/* Survives session restarts, like the agent's MIB. */
+	/*
+	 * Survives session restarts, like the agent's MIB. A port OMCI
+	 * deleted leaves the set even when the table write failed: the agent
+	 * drops the ME either way, and gpon_restore_omci_gems() would enable
+	 * the port again on every OMCC up.
+	 */
 	mutex_lock(&priv->gem_mib_lock);
 	__assign_bit(gem_port_id, priv->omci_gems, valid);
 	mutex_unlock(&priv->gem_mib_lock);
+	if (ret)
+		return ret;
 
 	gpon_refresh_netdev_link(priv, false);
 	dev_info(priv->dev,
